      <h2>Standards and References</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Methods are cited by designation and edition. Obtain the current edition from the issuing
          body and confirm the edition printed on any report.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>ASTM D1762</strong> — Chemical Analysis of Wood Charcoal. ASTM International.</li>
          <li><strong>ASTM D3172 / D3173 / D3174 / D3175</strong> — proximate analysis practice, moisture, ash and volatile matter for coal and coke. ASTM International.</li>
          <li><strong>ASTM D5865</strong> — Gross Calorific Value of Coal and Coke. ASTM International.</li>
          <li><strong>ASTM D440</strong> — Drop Shatter Test for Coal. ASTM International.</li>
          <li><strong>ISO 18122 / 18123 / 18125 / 18134</strong> — Solid biofuels: ash, volatile matter, calorific value and moisture. ISO.</li>
          <li><strong>ISO 17828 / 17831-1</strong> — Solid biofuels: bulk density and mechanical durability. ISO.</li>
          <li><strong>ISO 19867-1</strong> — Clean cookstoves: harmonised laboratory test protocols. ISO.</li>
          <li><strong>ISO/IEC 17025</strong> — Competence of testing and calibration laboratories. ISO and IEC.</li>
          <li><strong>GB/T 12496 (series)</strong> — Test methods of wooden activated carbon. Standardization Administration of China.</li>
          <li><strong>EN 1860-2</strong> — Barbecue charcoal and barbecue charcoal briquettes. European Committee for Standardization.</li>
          <li><strong>UN 1361</strong> — Carbon, animal or vegetable origin. UN Model Regulations; Class 4.2 in the IMDG Code.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
